kantSlotT -- SEQ 680 (Clay's dispatch of 2026-11-17, on Tony's rulings SEQ 677-679): the kant jitter's step 5, values out of the fields.
A SLOT LOCAL is a local the resolve pass is sure of (a count or a number, its first writer a top-level statement) that no native
store of a number into a count reaches anywhere; it lives in an alloca. A native read is a load and a native store a store, with
the = operator's irWIDE text first when a count goes into a number slot; no go-on check after it, and its target field goes to
%last. Before a testOP statement the slots stored natively since the last read are written back (a flag per slot says which);
after it every slot is read again from its field; the normal end writes back and then irSeqEnd; a stop after a testOP statement
leaves with no write-back. Each literal is read once, at the top of the function.
pop.sh runs this file twice: as written, and with the top-level kwCompile( and kwRan( lines deleted (the twin, all interpreted).
Stdout from SL ROW1 to SL END must be equal.
    ROW1   slRd: an if that prints is on testOP and changes s; the native t = s + 1 after it reads 12, the reloaded slot.
    ROW2   slPr: three native stores, then print reads the written-back fields.
    ROW3   slRet: return y on testOP hands back 16.
    ROW4   slWid: x is a number slot; x = 2 widens by ='s irWIDE (data, incant/setup) and stores 2.0.
    ROW5   slNar: c = 2.5 is a native number into a count, so c is no slot and keeps step 4's text (it rounds to 3).
    ROW6   slMix: a is handed to a call, so not sure and no slot; its stores stay field stores with their go-on checks,
           beside the slots s and i in the same native loop.
    ROW7   slNest: j's first writer is inside the loop, so j is no slot; i and s are.
    ROW8   slMid: a callee reads x and y by name before the return: the write-back made them 6 and 7.
    ROW9   slRef: a callee reads i and s by name after the native loop; then h = g + 1 refuses (g was cleared), on testOP,
           and the action stops there (7 statements of 9). A refused NATIVE field store, as the dispatch asked, cannot be
           built: opAssign of a boxed count or number never refuses, so the go-on check after a non-slot store is never
           taken. A refusal inside a callee does not stop its caller (measured, both sides), so the refusal is slRef's own.
    ROW10  slBy: a callee writes 9 into a by name; a is not stored after the read, so it is not written back: 9 4 10.
    ROW11  each called again.
    SL M5  FOUND, NOT RULED, pinned as read: a callee writes a group into the slot local a by name. a is not written back
           (it was not stored after the read), so the group survives on both sides (slG); the native read of a after the
           call still misreads, as sureKindT's M5 does (4 where the twin reads slG1).
    A local read by name from the top level AFTER its action returns echoes its tag on both sides; the activation is over.
    So every read of a local here is by a callee during the call.
    SL RAN <action> <statements run> testOP <calls>; SL NATIVE <action> then 1 native / 0 testOP per top-level statement.
    SL TEXT: slMix's compiled text, counted by pop.sh.
Action names here avoid the jitter's own local names (jitterNameShadows).
